<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Takht-e Soleyman</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Takht-e Soleyman is an archaeological site in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> province, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, built around a spring-fed lake on a hilltop. It held the main Sasanian sanctuary of the sacred fire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adur-gushnasp">Adur Gushnasp</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> sacked in 624, and in the thirteenth century the Ilkhanid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> built a summer palace there. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takht-e-soleyman.jpg" alt="Takht-e Soleyman" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elaheabed · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takhte_Soleyman_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Takht-e Soleymān, Persian for Throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, lies midway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urmia">Urmia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, very near the present-day town of Takab and 400 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>. The fortified site stands on a hill created by the outflow of a calcium-rich spring pond. It appears on the Peutinger Map. The biblical name came after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Muslim conquest of Persia</a> in the 7th century. Folk legend says King Solomon imprisoned monsters in a nearby crater about 100 m deep, called Zendan-e Soleyman, the Prison of Solomon, and that he created the flowing pond.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations have revealed traces of an occupation of the 5th century BCE in the Achaemenid era and later Parthian settlements in the citadel. Coins of the Sasanian shahs and of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> have also been found. The citadel holds the remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adur-gushnasp">Adur Gushnasp</a>, a Zoroastrian fire temple of the Sasanian era. It housed one of the three Great Fires, or Royal Fires, before which Sasanian rulers humbled themselves in order to take the throne, and was dedicated to the warrior class.</p><p>The Ilkhanid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> (r. 1265-82), a son of Hülagü, chose the site for a summer and hunting palace, partly because of the large ruins of the old temple and palace, whose layout he could use. Building probably began in 1271 and was completed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghun</a>. The choice also helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanids</a>, who had not been Muslim at first, to present themselves as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s history. The palace stood within an oval fortified wall, around a courtyard with an artificial lake, with four iwans and polygonal chambers. An audience hall with a large dome stood on the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">fire temple</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Ilkhanid complex is the only surviving secular structure of that period. Its walls carried painted stucco and lustre tiles in the lajvardina technique. Tiles show dragons and phoenixes alongside scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>, such as Fereydun defeating Zahhak, and quotations from the Qur'an. A stucco plate found in the southern octagonal chamber, now in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, carries a 42 cm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqarnas">muqarnas</a> plan, one of the earliest known Islamic architectural plans of its kind.</p><p>The British explorers Theodore and Mabel Bent dug at the site in early May 1889. Takht-e Soleymān was recognised as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in July 2003, and a small museum shows finds from it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Takht-e Soleymān” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its kings ruled from Ganzak, near the later fire temple of <b>Takht-e Soleyman</b>, over a land of horse pastures and mountains.</blockquote><a href="/state/media-atropatene">Media Atropatene</a></li></ul></section>
